$ Dyall.pvtz.unc
$
$ Description
$
$ Internet Archive for Theoretical Chemistry Accounts
$ ===================================================
$ 
$ Title: Relativistic and nonrelativistic finite nucleus optimized triple zeta
$        basis sets for the 4p, 5p and 6p elements.
$ 
$ Author: Kenneth G. Dyall
$ 
$ Date: 2 June 2002
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the basis sets described in the above article. The outermost d function in the
$ SCF exponent set is replaced by the two valence correlating d functions. 
$ In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF
$ exponent set.
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents and SCF coefficients for nonrelativistic wave functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d and f functions, relativistic and nonrelativistic.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of valence correlating functions, relativistic and nonrelativistic.
$ 
$ 6. Exponents of core correlating functions, relativistic and nonrelativistic.
$ 
$ 7. Exponents of original SCF outer d function, before replacement by the
$    valence correlating d functions.
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
$ functions. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ 1. vtz, vcor (equivalent to cc-pvtz): add the first and third outermost
$    s and p primitives from the SCF exponent set, the outermost two
$    d primitives and the valence correlating f function to the SCF set.
$    (HF + 2s2p2d1f valence)
$ 
$    This is the cc-pvtz equivalent, but has no core relaxation, which can
$    be important for the heavy elements.
$ 
$ 2. c+vtz, vcor (adding outer core relaxation and polarization): To basis set 1,
$    add the fourth and sixth outermost s and p primitives and the third and fourth 
$    outermost d primitives from the SCF exponent set, and add the dz polarizing 
$    f function or the geometric mean of the outermost core f correlating function 
$    and the valence f correlating function.
$    (HF + 2s2p2d1f valence + 2s2p2d1f core)
$ 
$    This basis set is preferable to basis set 2 because it permits the (n-1) shell 
$    to be polarized.
$ 
$ 3. c+vtz, c+vcor (adding outer core correlation): To basis set 1, add the fourth
$    and sixth outermost s and p primitives and the third and fourth outermost d 
$    primitives from the SCF exponent set, and add the core correlating 2f1g functions.
$    (HF + 2s2p2d1f valence + 2s2p2d2f1g core)
$ 
$ 4. c+vtz, c+vcor, cpol (adding core polarization and corelation): To basis set 3,
$    add the polarizing f function used in basis set 2.
$ 
$ 5. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d and f functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$
$  REFERENCE
$ K. G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. 108 (2002) 335.
a 31
